\description{
The same 2,000 NELS:88 eighth graders as \link{disc}, in the same order, with
continuous predictors: the base-year socioeconomic status composite
(\code{ses}), reading and mathematics test scores from 1988 (grade 8), and the
reading score from the 1990 follow-up (grade 10). One row per student.
The other variables are recodes with readable names: being sent to the
office for misbehaving, sex, home language, own bedroom, parents'
education and race/ethnicity.
}
\details{
\strong{Coding.} All the recodes are 0/1 except \code{osentoff} (0 = never, 1 =
once or twice, 2 = more than twice) and the factor \code{race}, which has the
same five levels as in \link{disc}, with Asian as the first (reference)
level. \code{fath_ed} and \code{moth_ed} mean the parent finished high school;
\code{fath_coll} and \code{moth_coll} mean the parent graduated from college.
\code{notengl} is 1 for the 220 students whose home language is not English;
the 1,560 students who legitimately skipped the NELS home-language item
are coded 0, together with the English speakers. \code{minority} is 1 for
Asian, Hispanic, Black and American Indian students alike.

\strong{Scores.} \code{reading1} and \code{math1} (grade 8, 1988) and \code{reading2}
(grade 10, 1990) are standardized test scores with mean about 50 and SD
about 10, so a coefficient per 10 points is roughly a coefficient per
SD. \code{ses} is the NELS base-year SES composite, built from parents'
education and occupations and family income; it is on a standardized
scale (here from -2.9 to 1.9, SD 0.8).

\strong{Missing data.} \code{reading2} is NA for 218 students, 215 of whom did not
complete the 1990 test; \code{reading1} and \code{math1} are NA for 77 and 75
students, and \code{ses} for 1. As in \link{disc}, parents' education is unknown
for 282 fathers and 208 mothers. These values did not change in 1.0.0.

\strong{Version 1.0.0.} As in \link{disc}, \code{race} gained the level American Indian
for 68 students who were NA before; the 24 nonrespondents are NA.
\code{minority} already counted these 68 students as 1. No other values
changed.

\strong{Suggested analyses.}
\itemize{
\item logistic regression of \code{sentoff} on continuous predictors such as
\code{ses}, \code{math1} and \code{reading1}, with odds ratios per unit or per 10
points;
\item comparing \code{ses} with the parents' education dummies as measures of
family background;
\item linear regression of \code{reading2} on \code{reading1} and other predictors, a
simple model of change between grade 8 and grade 10;
\item an interaction between a dummy and a continuous predictor, such as
\code{male} by \code{ses}.
